      "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3165126e7211148f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEngland designates 34 Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs), covering approximately 15% of its land area and protecting a variety of landscapes from coastal zones to inland hills.[130] These areas are primarily clustered in the south and west, reflecting historical emphases on safeguarding rural and coastal scenery in those regions, with fewer designations in the north and east.[1] The Cotswolds AONB stands as the largest, extending over 2,038 km² across six counties including Gloucestershire and Oxfordshire.[131] Delistings remain unprecedented in England, underscoring the enduring status of designations once established; boundary modifications, however, have occurred through periodic reviews, such as the 2020 extension adding 38 km² to the Suffolk Coast and Heaths AONB.[132] The designated AONBs in England are:\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3165126e7211148f\">>>",
